<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/how-historians-work">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in History, and it is deliberately the first one: before any
    king, war or revolution can be studied, you need the method that turns leftover
    fragments into justified claims. You need nothing from earlier lessons — no dates, no
    names, no prior reading. What you will build here is a set of questions you can point
    at any source, from a Bronze-Age ledger to yesterday's social-media post.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what history is — and why it is not simply "the past";</li>
    <li>tell primary from secondary sources, and explain why the labels are about role, not reliability;</li>
    <li>interrogate a source with the critic's questions — author, audience, intent, context — and judge it source by source;</li>
    <li>use biased sources honestly, and read silences as evidence;</li>
    <li>distinguish a trigger from a cause, and explain why every historical conclusion is provisional.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit to carry through the whole subject: never ask only <em>what does this say?</em>
    but always also <em>why does this exist?</em> That second question is where history
    begins.
  </p>

  <h2>The past is gone; the evidence remains</h2>
  <p>
    History is not the past. It is <strong>the disciplined study of the past through its
    surviving evidence</strong>. The distinction matters more than it first appears. Nobody
    can observe the signing of the Treaty of Versailles or hear a crowd in a medieval
    square as it happened; the events themselves are unreachable. What remains are
    fragments — documents, artefacts, coins, photographs, recordings — each one produced
    by someone, for a purpose, and each one surviving by accident or by the decisions of
    archivists who thought it worth keeping. Most of the past left nothing at all: the
    illiterate majority, the ordinary days, the conversations that were never written down.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the historian's craft is reasoning from fragments to claims, under real uncertainty.
    A useful analogy is the courtroom. A jury never saw the crime; it reasons from
    testimony and exhibits, and it is trained to distrust exactly the things that seem
    most convincing — a confident eyewitness, a vivid story. Historians work the same way:
    everything stands on <strong>sources</strong>, sources are interrogated rather than
    trusted, and single pieces of evidence are checked against each other before any
    conclusion is drawn. What separates history from myth or memory is that the reasoning
    is exposed, where others can check and challenge it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the past is gone and the evidence is partial, produced with
    purposes of its own. The historian's job — like the jury's — is disciplined reasoning
    from imperfect witnesses. Now we meet the witnesses and learn to question them.
  </p>

  <h2>Four steps from artefact to argument</h2>
  <p>
    The working loop of the discipline has four moves, and this page walks through each in
    turn.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-step method from artefact to argument" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps go from raw material to finished argument,
    and each step answers the previous one's weakness. Primary sources are partial, so we
    criticise them; criticism of one source is never enough, so we corroborate; even
    corroborated evidence underdetermines a conclusion, so we interpret — openly, with the
    frame declared. Notice also what is <em>absent</em>: any step where the historian just
    believes the source.
  </p>

  <h2>Primary and secondary sources</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>primary source</strong> is a gateway to the past: an object or document
    produced <em>at or near the time</em> being studied, by people with direct connection
    to the events. A soldier's letter from the Somme, a parliamentary transcript, a census
    return, a coin, a manor's account roll, a photograph, a recorded interview — all
    primary. A <strong>secondary source</strong> is produced later, by someone interpreting
    primary material: a textbook, a biography, the very page you are reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction is about proximity and role, not age or authority. A 1960s memoir about
    1916 is a primary source <em>for 1960s memory</em> and a secondary source <em>for
    1916</em> — same document, two roles. And proximity is a trade-off, not a prize.
    Primary sources give direct access but were never written for you: a tax roll was
    written to collect revenue, not to describe the economy. Secondary sources give
    synthesis and context, but embed someone else's selection and interpretation — so they
    must themselves be checked. Between the two sits the <strong>tertiary</strong> layer of
    compilations like encyclopedias: fine for orientation, but a springboard to better
    sources rather than citable evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    One worked example shows how much context carries. The building now called the Hagia
    Sophia was built in Constantinople in the sixth century as a church — its gold mosaics
    and vast dome declared the power of emperor Justinian and the glory of his God to a
    mostly illiterate world. In 1453 the city fell to the Ottomans, and Sultan Mehmed II,
    recognising the building's beauty, had it converted into a mosque; minarets rose at its
    corners, Arabic calligraphy was set beside the Greek mosaics. Centuries later it became
    a museum, and recently a mosque again. The building is a primary source throughout —
    but what it is evidence <em>of</em> depends entirely on which layer you are reading,
    and knowing the sequence is the difference between reading the building and merely
    looking at it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "primary" means reliable and "secondary" means second-hand
  and weaker. It seems reasonable because "primary" sounds like "first-hand truth". But a
  diary written the evening of an event is primary and may be self-serving; a modern
  historian's careful statistical reconstruction is secondary and may be far more accurate
  about the overall pattern. Reliability is judged source by source, never by type.</p>

  <h2>Source criticism: interrogating the witness</h2>
  <p>
    Before using any source, historians interrogate it. The traditional split is two-fold.
    <strong>External criticism</strong> asks: is it genuine? Is this letter really signed by
    Lincoln; is this "medieval" charter a later forgery; do the paper, ink, language and
    handwriting fit the claimed date? (The <em>Donation of Constantine</em>, which for
    centuries seemed to grant the pope vast temporal power, was exposed as an eighth-century
    forgery partly by linguistic analysis — its Latin looked nothing like fourth-century
    usage.) <strong>Internal criticism</strong> asks: what does it mean and how far can it be
    trusted? Working historians compress this into four questions you can memorise:
    <em>author, audience, intent, context</em>. Who wrote it and what was their background;
    for whom was it meant, public or private; why was it written — record, persuasion, or
    self-justification; and what was happening when it was made?
  </p>
  <p>
    The questions matter because different sources tell the truth about different things.
    A royal proclamation tells you what the state wanted its subjects to believe; a
    soldier's letter what one literate man chose to tell his family; a merchant's ledger
    what someone counted carefully because money depended on it. Purpose is the master
    question: every source is an artefact of an intention. Propaganda posters are honest
    about being propaganda, but the subtle cases matter more — the victory memoir that
    downplays failures, the court chronicle that flatters its patron, the plain photograph
    that still involves choices of framing and timing. Asking "why does this source exist?"
    converts a passive quotation into evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Bias, corroboration and reading against the grain</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bias</strong> in everyday speech means prejudice; in source work it is broader —
    every source has a <em>position</em>, a point of view with blind spots. The historian's
    aim is not to find unbiased sources (there are none) but to understand each source's
    position well enough to compensate for it. Two techniques do most of the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first is <strong>corroboration</strong>: one source is a claim; independent sources
    agreeing move it toward fact. Where sources <em>conflict</em> is often where the
    interesting history lives — whose account of the same battle differs, and why? The
    second is <strong>reading against the grain</strong>: extracting information the source
    never intended to give. The tax rolls that never name the peasants still reveal, in
    aggregate, how many households there were, what they owed, and when famine thinned the
    numbers. Silences are evidence too: the history of the enslaved, of women, of the
    illiterate majority must often be reconstructed from the records of the people who
    owned, governed and wrote about them — which is itself a fact about power.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a biased source cannot be used. This feels right because
  bias sounds like contamination. But a biased source can be <em>perfect</em> evidence —
  of the bias. Wartime propaganda films tell you little about accurate casualty figures
  and a great deal about how a government wanted its people to see the war, which is a
  legitimate and important historical question. The sin is not using a biased source; it
  is using one without saying what for.</p>

  <h2>Arguing from evidence: causation, not chronology</h2>
  <p>
    History's central intellectual act is the <strong>causal argument</strong>: not just
    what happened, but why. And its central discipline is that <strong>"earlier than" is not
    "because of"</strong>. The Latin tag is <em>post hoc ergo propter hoc</em> — "after
    this, therefore because of this" — and resisting it is both the field's original sin
    and its ever-present temptation. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in June
    1914 preceded the First World War, but saying it "caused" the war needs an argument:
    through what mechanism, given which other conditions? Historians therefore separate the
    <strong>trigger</strong> (the spark), the <strong>preconditions</strong> (long-term
    tensions that made a spark dangerous) and <strong>contingency</strong> (the role of
    decisions and chance along the way). A good causal argument weighs these explicitly and
    tests itself against <em>counterfactuals</em>: if the alliance system had not existed,
    would the Sarajevo shots still have produced a general European war?
  </p>
  <p>
    Interpretation enters inevitably, because <strong>evidence underdetermines
    conclusions</strong>. The same archive can support an economic explanation of the
    French Revolution or a political one; the data rarely force a single verdict. Honest
    historians therefore state their frame — economic, cultural, political, "history from
    below" — and argue for it. This is what <strong>historiography</strong> means: the study
    of how interpretations have changed, from the "Great Man" narratives of the nineteenth
    century, through Marxist emphases on economic structures, to today's cultural and
    global turns. "The facts speak for themselves" is the first warning sign of bad
    history; facts speak only when interrogated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> sources are questioned for genuineness and purpose, checked
    against each other, and finally organised into causal arguments whose frames are
    declared. Two judging tools remain — how to grade a source, and how strongly to hold a
    conclusion.
  </p>

  <h2>Reliability, utility and provisional conclusions</h2>
  <p>
    Two evaluative axes are often confused, and keeping them apart dissolves half of all
    weak source analysis. <strong>Reliability</strong> asks: can this source be believed
    about what it claims? A physician's 1348 account of plague symptoms is highly reliable
    on symptoms (he saw them), far less so on causes (he blamed a celestial conjunction).
    <strong>Utility</strong> asks: how useful is this source for <em>this</em> question? The
    same unreliable chronicle may be superb evidence of how its author's monastery wished
    to be remembered. Utility depends on the question; reliability does not. This is why
    "limited value" is never a complete judgement of a source — limited value <em>for
    what?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, every historical conclusion is <strong>provisional</strong> — not as a humble
    gesture but for a hard reason: new evidence can always appear. The opening of Soviet
    archives after 1991 rewrote scholarship on the early Cold War within a decade; the
    recovery of the <em>Vindolanda</em> writing tablets transformed knowledge of ordinary
    Roman frontier life from almost nothing to remarkable detail. Historians also
    increasingly work with sources earlier generations ignored: oral history,
    archaeobotany, shipwrecks, ice cores. The discipline's confidence lies in its method,
    not in any fixed conclusion.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> if historians keep revising each other, history must be
  mere opinion. The revision looks like weakness because science lessons teach that settled
  facts do not change. But historians disagree mainly where the evidence is thin, and every
  revision happens in public, against criteria — evidence, logic, coverage — that the next
  historian can apply again. Contrast history with propaganda: propaganda cannot lose an
  argument, because it never states the grounds. A discipline whose claims can lose is
  exactly the opposite of "just opinion".</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A tax collector's roll from 1320 lists every household in a village. Give one thing it is strong evidence for, and one thing it cannot tell you.</li>
    <li>Why can a biased propaganda film still be excellent historical evidence?</li>
    <li>The Archduke was assassinated in June 1914; the war began in July. What more does a causal claim need beyond that sequence?</li>
    <li>A chronicle is wildly unreliable about a battle. Does that make it useless? Explain using reliability and utility.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Strong for: how many households existed, what each owed,
    how the numbers moved year to year (famine, plague). Cannot tell you: why individual
    families were rich or poor, what villagers thought, or anything about households the
    collector missed — the roll records what the state needed, nothing more. (2) Because
    utility depends on the question: it is poor evidence of what happened and superb
    evidence of what a government wanted its people to believe — which is itself a
    historical fact. (3) A mechanism and conditions: how the killing activated the alliance
    system, which preconditions made a local crisis escalate, and what contingency did —
    "after" alone never establishes "because". (4) No. Reliability is low on the battle,
    but utility is judged per question: the same chronicle may be first-rate evidence of
    its monastery's politics, language or values.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>History is the disciplined study of the past through surviving evidence — reasoning exposed where others can check it.</li>
    <li>Primary sources come from (or near) the time; secondary sources interpret them; tertiary compilations are springboards, not evidence.</li>
    <li>Criticise every source: external (is it genuine?) and internal — author, audience, intent, context.</li>
    <li>Corroborate: one source is a claim; independent agreement moves it toward fact; conflicts are leads, not obstacles.</li>
    <li>Bias is a position to compensate for, and a biased source can be perfect evidence of the bias.</li>
    <li>Separate trigger from preconditions from contingency; "earlier than" is not "because of".</li>
    <li>Reliability is about belief; utility is about your question; both are judged source by source.</li>
    <li>All conclusions are provisional because new evidence can always surface — that is the method working, not failing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Every later lesson in this subject is an exercise in the loop you just learned. When you
    meet the revolutions of 1848 or the unification of Italy and Germany, you will be doing
    exactly this: weighing a proclamation against a letter against a census, separating
    triggers from preconditions, and noticing which interpretation each textbook chose. The
    method also leaves the classroom with you — the same questions judge a news report, a
    viral claim or an advertisement, which is why this lesson may be the most practical one
    in the whole atlas.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two philosophical problems sit underneath the craft. First, <strong>presentism</strong>:
    judging the past by present values. Sometimes it is unavoidable and right — no one
    should describe slavery in neutral administrative language. But presentism can also
    blind: reading ancient motives through modern categories (was Alexander "imperialist"
    in our sense?) invents anachronistic minds. The working compromise most historians
    adopt: <em>understand</em> the past on its own terms, and <em>evaluate</em> it where a
    shared moral floor exists, while saying openly which you are doing. Second, the
    <strong>objectivity debate</strong>: E. H. Carr's 1961 <em>What Is History?</em> argued
    that the historian is a product of their own society, so pure objectivity is a myth —
    yet Carr still insisted facts constrain, that some accounts are better supported than
    others. The modern settlement holds that line: interpretations are frameworks, evidence
    is the court, and the difference between history and propaganda is that history can
    lose arguments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Method at the frontier is increasingly quantitative and scientific.
    <em>Prosopography</em> reconstructs the lives of groups from scattered records;
    <em>climate history</em> reads tree rings and ice cores to connect harvest failures to
    revolts; digital corpora let scholars track the diffusion of a single idea across
    thousands of pamphlets. The wider information world has even imported the method
    wholesale: fact-checkers now teach a protocol called <strong>SIFT</strong> — stop,
    investigate the source, find better coverage, trace the claim to its original context —
    which is source criticism and corroboration renamed for the internet age, including its
    habit of "reading laterally": judging a website not by reading it harder, but by
    leaving it to see what other sources say about it. None of the new tools escapes the
    old loop in the widget: source, criticism, corroboration, openly stated interpretation.
    The tools change; the discipline is the loop.
  </p>
  <p>
    One worked demonstration binds the whole lesson together, because it uses every tool at
    once. Consider the claim "medieval people believed the Earth was flat". A student of
    the method starts asking the source questions immediately. Who said this, and when? The
    belief turns out to be a nineteenth-century invention — popularised by Washington
    Irving's fictionalised biography of Columbus (1828) and by the Draper–White "conflict"
    narrative of science versus religion — a modern prejudice read back onto the past,
    which is textbook presentism. What did the period itself say? Primary sources say the
    opposite: Bede describes the Earth as round in the eighth century, the spherical Earth
    is standard university doctrine through the Middle Ages, and Columbus's dispute with
    his critics was over the Earth's <em>size</em>, not its shape — his critics, on this
    point, were closer to correct. How did the error survive? By circulation without
    criticism: an anecdote repeated from textbook to textbook is a secondary source quoting
    a secondary source, the exact failure mode corroboration exists to catch. The
    flat-earth myth is a small thing, and that is the point — a method that catches it is
    worth trusting with the larger questions, from the causes of wars to the everyday
    lives the archives barely recorded.
  </p>

